WEBVTT

NOTE Draft timings from the script (150 words a minute). Re-time against the recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Drink Wars is a strategy game
about running a craft brewery.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.800
You and your rivals sell to the same
shoppers, and every round you make the same

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:17.200
kinds of decisions a real brewery makes:
what to charge, how much to brew, what to

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.000
build, and how to pay for it.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.200
A round is one quarter of a year.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.200
This video walks through one round from
start to finish, one screen at a time.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.000
It shows what each panel tells
you and where each decision goes.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.600
The decisions are yours.

9
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.600
Across the top is the instrument bar:
the round you are in, your cash, your

10
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.200
capacity, and your rank.

11
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.400
Cash is what the brewery holds right now.

12
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.800
Capacity is how many drinks your
tanks can brew this quarter.

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.000
Rank appears once the first round has run.

14
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Below it are three tabs.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.200
Decide is where you set this round's plan.

16
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.000
Review is where you read what happened.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.800
The third is the map, which becomes City
and Globe when the game has more than one

18
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.000
market.

19
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.800
On the right of Decide is the Boardroom:
four officers who sum up the firm for you.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.800
The CEO covers the standings.

21
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.400
The CMO covers demand.

22
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.800
The COO covers operations and cost.

23
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.800
The CFO covers the money.

24
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.000
Each one has a details link that
shows the numbers behind the summary.

25
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.800
In a class game, each officer
is a seat on your team.

26
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.800
Start with the CMO, who reports how many
shoppers the market has this quarter, by

27
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.000
category.

28
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.400
Lagers and Light and Craft Premium are the
two categories you start with, and a new

29
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.200
category can open later in the season.

30
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.200
Shoppers in each category weigh
price, quality and brand differently.

31
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.200
After the first round, the CMO also shows
your share, and the prices every rival

32
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.800
charged the round before.

33
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.400
The production lines are where prices go:
one price per drink in each category.

34
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.000
Under each box is what one drink
earns over what it costs to brew.

35
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.800
Price is the lever that moves fastest.

36
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.600
A change shows up the same quarter.

37
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.800
Tanks set how many drinks you can brew.

38
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.800
An order in the Tanks card adds tanks
that arrive next quarter, not this one.

39
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.200
The card shows how many drinks an order
adds, what your tanks will be next quarter,

40
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.600
and what an idle tank still costs, because
upkeep is due whether a tank brews or

41
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.800
not.

42
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.000
Tanks also wear down a
little every quarter.

43
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
The allocation bar splits this
quarter's tanks between the categories.

44
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.400
Below that is Build the Brewery.

45
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.800
Spending here builds stocks that last:
recipe quality, brand, brewing operations,

46
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.000
which lowers your cost per drink, and
your standing with staff, investors and

47
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.200
regulators.

48
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.200
A stock grows over the quarters after you
spend, and it wears down without upkeep.

49
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.800
These are slow levers.

50
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.000
What you spend this round shows up later.

51
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.600
The Financing card is
money in and money out.

52
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.600
You can borrow from the bank, sell shares
to investors, repay debt, or pay a

53
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.800
dividend.

54
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.400
Each has a limit.

55
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.000
Those limits are on the right, in the
commit rail, under Cash and credit.

56
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.800
It lists what you have to work with this
quarter: cash on hand, what the bank will

57
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.800
lend, what investors will put
in, and the emergency overdraft.

58
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.800
The overdraft is a last
resort at a penalty rate.

59
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.400
A firm that ends a quarter past it closes.

60
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.000
Under that is projected cash: what your
plan leaves before a single drink sells.

61
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.800
Then come the costs that are paid
from sales whatever you sell.

62
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.400
Operating costs cover overhead,
tank upkeep, rent and staff.

63
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:21.200
Interest is its own line: the rate this
quarter on the debt you will hold after this

64
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.800
round's loan or repayment.

65
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.200
Break-even is how many drinks
cover those costs at your prices.

66
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.200
When the plan is set, you
press Brew and Resolve.

67
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.200
If the plan spends more than your cash
and financing can cover, a check appears

68
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.400
first.

69
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:47.600
It lists exactly what the round would cut,
so you can change the plan or send it as

70
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:48.800
it is.

71
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:55.200
Your rivals decide at the same time, and
none of you sees the others' plans until

72
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.400
the round runs.

73
00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.400
In a class game, each seat
submits its own desk.

74
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.000
After you submit, the rail confirms what
was sent and which teammates are in.

75
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.200
The round runs when your
instructor locks it.

76
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.800
The results page comes first: where you
finished, your net income, your cash, and

77
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.200
what hit the market this round.

78
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.600
Each number traces back to a choice, yours
or a rival's, or to the market itself.

79
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.200
Review holds the detail.

80
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.800
Statements and Ratios has the income statement,
the balance sheet and the cash flow.

81
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.600
Revenue minus the cost of the
drinks you sold is gross profit.

82
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.200
Subtract operating costs to get
operating income, then interest.

83
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.200
The Value stick tab shows, for each category,
your price and your cost per drink,

84
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.800
straight from your books.

85
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.200
Above them sits what shoppers would
pay, called willingness to pay.

86
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:60.000
No shopper tells you that number, and it
differs from one shopper to the next, so it

87
00:05:60.000 --> 00:06:04.000
shows as a question mark until
you buy market research.

88
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
The survey then estimates it for a typical
shopper and gives the range for most

89
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.200
shoppers.

90
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.800
Below your cost is what your suppliers and
staff need, called willingness to sell.

91
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.400
The firm keeps the gap
between price and cost.

92
00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.600
The Dispatch is the round's news: market
events, rivals' public moves, and notices

93
00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.200
only your firm sees.

94
00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.800
That is one round.

95
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.600
Every round after it works the same
way: read, decide, brew, review.

96
00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.800
To try it before class, open Drink
Wars and choose Learn the game.

97
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:48.000
It runs this same round as a practice game,
with a guide you can close at any time.
